### Local build caches ###
#
# Some of these are repository-local on purpose, some are only leftovers. All are safe to delete;
# what differs is what it costs you.
#
#   .nuget/    deliberate. scripts/publish-desktop-win-x64.ps1 points NUGET_PACKAGES here so a
#              publish cannot pick up packages from another checkout. Deleting it re-downloads.
#   .gocache/  deliberate. deploy/go-server/build-linux.ps1 points GOCACHE at a workspace path.
#              Deleting it only costs a slower next build.
#   .gomodcache/ must NOT be here, and the same script refuses to build if GOMODCACHE resolves
#              below the repository: Go marks extracted modules read-only, which makes the
#              workspace painful to delete. The pattern stays so a stray one is never committed.
#   .m2/       not the live repository. Maven resolves from the user-level ~/.m2 (visible in any
#              surefire classpath), so this is residue from an ad-hoc run and is pure dead weight.
#   /.tmp/     scratch from integration runs: dependencies compiled from source, migration smoke
#              tests. Nothing here is reused between runs.
#
# The rest are per-tool home directories created by whatever was run in this folder.
.appdata/
.dotnet/
.dotnet-home/
.m2/
.npm-cache/
**/.npm-cache/
/.tmp/
.nuget/
.gocache/
.gomodcache/
**/.gocache/
**/.gomodcache/
**/.gocache-codex/
**/.gomodcache-codex/
**/.gradle/
**/.gradle-user-home/
**/BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts/
BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
# Local agent tooling. Ignored wholesale rather than per-file: the previous rules named
# config.toml and one skill directory, so a later skill was committed without anyone noticing.
/.codex/
/.zcode/
local.properties
**/bin/
**/obj/

